<header class="sp-head"><p class="featured-region">5 October 1877 · Bear Paw Mountains, Montana</p><h1 class="sp-title">Chief Joseph: I Will Fight No More Forever</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chief-joseph">Chief Joseph</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>After a fighting retreat of some 1,900 kilometres, the Nez Perce, trying to reach refuge in Canada, were surrounded by the United States Army just short of the border. Their leader Joseph surrendered.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The words</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>Hear me, my chiefs! I am tired; my heart is sick and sad. From where the sun now stands I will fight no more forever.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">As reported by Lieutenant Charles Erskine Scott Wood, who may have shaped the wording.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>Despite promises that they could return home, the Nez Perce were exiled to Indian Territory, where many died. Joseph spent the rest of his life petitioning for his people's return to their homeland, and died on a reservation in 1904.</p></section>
